{
  // Everything in this file is public: it is committed. The API key is NOT here.
  // It goes in as a secret, which is encrypted at rest and never shown again:
  //
  //     npx wrangler secret put HELIUS_API_KEY
  //
  // A key pasted into this file would be in git history, which is the exact
  // problem this worker exists to solve, one layer further down.
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "commish-rpc",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-09-07",

  /* THE CUSTOM DOMAIN, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES RATE LIMITING POSSIBLE AT ALL.
     Deploying with this provisions rpc.commish.fun — DNS record and
     certificate — inside the commish.fun zone. That matters because the
     `ratelimits` binding further down is inert on *.workers.dev, which
     Cloudflare treats as a Free website outside your zones, and a WAF Rate
     Limiting Rule can only be attached to a zone. So the domain is the
     prerequisite for the one control that actually works.

     THE OLD HOSTNAME CLOSES ITSELF, AND THIS COMMENT SAID THE OPPOSITE.

     It claimed workers_dev defaults to true, so adding a route would leave
     commish-rpc.<subdomain>.workers.dev running alongside, and that a second
     deploy with workers_dev:false would be needed to retire it. The bare
     default really is true — the configuration reference says so, which is
     where that claim came from — but the routing page carries the rule that
     actually applies here: "If you do not specify workers_dev = false but add
     a routes component to your Wrangler configuration file, the value of
     workers_dev will be inferred as false on the next deploy."
     (/workers/configuration/routing/workers-dev/). Two pages, one of them
     answering the question. Deploying this config retired the old hostname on
     the spot.

     Observed, minutes after the first deploy with this route:

       commish-rpc.<subdomain>.workers.dev  -> 404, error code 1042
       rpc.commish.fun                      -> 200 {"result":"ok"}

     Good outcome, wrong prediction — and the wrong prediction cost something,
     because the comment then laid out a four-step order designed around a
     window that never existed. NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL was still pointing at the
     workers.dev hostname when it went dark, so the live site lost every chain
     read until Vercel was updated. The hazard was real and correctly
     described; it simply arrives at step 1 rather than step 3.

     SO THE ORDER IS: change NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL to https://rpc.commish.fun in
     Vercel and redeploy IN THE SAME SITTING as the deploy that adds this
     route. There is no safe gap between them to work in.

     BOTH ARE NOW WRITTEN OUT RATHER THAN INFERRED, and that is not tidiness.
     Cloudflare's own caution: "If you disable your workers.dev route in the
     Cloudflare dashboard but do not update your Worker's Wrangler file with
     workers_dev = false, the workers.dev route will be RE-ENABLED the next
     time you deploy your Worker with Wrangler." An inferred value is one
     `routes` edit away from silently reopening the exact bypass that makes the
     WAF rule meaningless, and it would reopen quietly, on a deploy about
     something else entirely. Explicit survives that.

     preview_urls matters for the same reason and is easy to miss: a preview
     URL is <version>-<name>.<subdomain>.workers.dev, which is also outside the
     zone and also unmetered by a WAF rule on it. Wrangler 4.44+ defaults it to
     match workers_dev, so it is false already; it is pinned here so that
     "already" does not depend on the version somebody deploys with. */
  "routes": [{ "pattern": "rpc.commish.fun", "custom_domain": true }],
  "workers_dev": false,
  "preview_urls": false,

  // Logs go to `wrangler tail`. The worker logs no request bodies and no
  // credentials, so this carries method names and status codes, which is what
  // you want when a screen starts calling something the allowlist refuses.
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  },

  "vars": {
    // devnet or mainnet. Defaulted in code as well, because a proxy that
    // quietly talks to mainnet because a variable was unset is a worse failure
    // than one that talks to the wrong test cluster.
    "HELIUS_CLUSTER": "devnet",

    // The origins allowed to use this proxy, compared whole rather than by
    // suffix. www is still here because Vercel still answers on it, as a
    // redirect; harmless, and it costs nothing to keep until that is retired.
    //
    // There is no wildcard and no default. The worker refuses every request
    // while this is empty, on purpose: an open proxy holding a live key is the
    // one thing that must not be what happens when somebody forgets a
    // variable.
    "CORS_ALLOW_ORIGIN": "https://commish.fun,https://www.commish.fun",

    // The one program getProgramAccounts may scan.
    //
    // THIS WAS COMMENTED OUT, ON THE REASONING THAT "the id belongs with the
    // deploy, not here", to be supplied as `--var PROGRAM_ID:<id>` at cutover.
    // That reasoning was wrong twice over.
    //
    // It was wrong in fact: the id is a constant. It comes from
    // target/deploy/commish-keypair.json, it is `declare_id!` in lib.rs, it is
    // baked into src/idl/commish.json, and it is the SAME on devnet and
    // mainnet. There is no per-deploy value to supply.
    //
    // And it was wrong in effect, which is the part that was measured rather
    // than argued. With it unset the guard in index.ts:153 is skipped entirely
    // and only the weaker "must carry filters" test at :156 runs. Probed
    // against the live worker on 2026-09-08, forging nothing but an Origin
    // header:
    //
    //   getProgramAccounts, someone else's program, no filters -> 403
    //   getProgramAccounts, someone else's program, WITH a filter -> 200 []
    //
    // So the deployed proxy would scan any program on Solana for anybody who
    // attached a dataSize filter, on our Helius key, with no working rate limit
    // in front of it. A protection that depends on somebody remembering a CLI
    // flag during a cutover is not a protection; it is a plan to have one.
    //
    // Here it survives every deploy. The `--var` in mainnet-cutover.sh still
    // passes the same value and simply agrees with this line.
    "PROGRAM_ID": "Adb5CFrY4vYiGQnTQ5qsaPPMUAWKyxwVAQtFHWcshjPa"
  },

  /* RATE LIMITING LIVES IN THE DASHBOARD NOW, NOT IN THESE BINDINGS.

     THE ENDPOINT IS PROTECTED. A WAF Rate Limiting Rule on the commish.fun
     zone does the work, and it was verified by measurement rather than by
     being switched on — 300 requests in 10 seconds from one IP:

       100 x 403   reached the worker (its own origin refusal)
       200 x 429   blocked at the edge

     and, because the method allowlist never applied to socket frames, the same
     test against websocket upgrades — 200 in 6 seconds:

        98 x 502   reached the worker; Helius refuses that many live sockets
       102 x 429   blocked at the edge

     Normal traffic recovers as soon as the mitigation window passes.

     THE RULE IS NOT IN THIS REPOSITORY, which is the thing to know before
     trusting or changing anything here. It is zone configuration:
     Security > Security rules > Rate limiting rules, matching URI Path eq "/"
     at 100 requests per 10 seconds, action Block, per IP.

     WHY IT MATCHES A PATH AND NOT A HOSTNAME, which looks like a mistake and
     is not. Free plan rate limiting can only use Path and Verified Bot in a
     rule expression; Host requires Pro. There is no hostname condition
     available to write. It is nonetheless correctly scoped, because
     rpc.commish.fun is the ONLY hostname in this zone that Cloudflare proxies:

       commish.fun      -> 64.29.17.65      Server: Vercel     (DNS only)
       www.commish.fun  -> 64.29.17.65      Server: Vercel     (DNS only)
       rpc.commish.fun  -> 104.21.34.91     Server: cloudflare (proxied)

     The WAF therefore never sees website traffic, and every request it does
     see is this proxy, arriving at "/" — both the JSON-RPC POSTs and the
     websocket upgrade.

     THE TRAP THAT CREATES: if commish.fun is ever switched to proxied (orange
     cloud), that rule instantly begins rate limiting the WEBSITE at 100
     requests per 10 seconds per IP. One page load pulls seventeen chunks plus
     fonts, so that is roughly five page loads before a shared office or
     household IP is blocked from the site itself. Before proxying the website,
     upgrade to Pro and add a hostname condition to that rule.

     THE BINDINGS BELOW STILL DO NOT WORK, and are kept for the reasons at the
     end of this comment. They attach correctly: `wrangler deploy --dry-run`
     lists them, a deployed worker sees RPC_LIMIT as an object, and
     env.RPC_LIMIT.limit() is callable and returns a result. It simply never
     denies.

     Measured, on a preview version uploaded with the limit dropped to 5 per 10
     seconds so the threshold could not be missed:
       - 15 sequential POSTs      -> 15 x 200, no 429
       - 10 websocket upgrades    -> 10 x 101, no 429
       - 12 direct limit() calls on ONE key -> 12 x {"success":true}

     The last line is the decisive one: the limiter itself returns success
     twelve times against a budget of five. This is not a keying mistake, not
     the documented per-location counter, and not eventual consistency at the
     margin. The binding is inert on this worker.

     The cause was that the Worker was served from *.workers.dev, which
     Cloudflare treats as a Free website outside your zones. That diagnosis
     held: moving to rpc.commish.fun and adding the WAF rule produced 429s
     immediately, as recorded at the top of this comment. The bindings
     themselves are STILL inert even on the custom domain — the 429s come from
     the zone rule, not from these — so they remain kept rather than trusted:
     they are correct, they cost nothing, and they would begin working if the
     platform ever honours them. Nothing should be built on the assumption that
     they do.

     Two budgets, because a POST and a socket are not worth the same upstream:
     a POST is billed once, a subscription is billed for as long as it is held.
     Note the zone rule cannot express that distinction — Free plan allows ONE
     rule, so both share the single 100-per-10s budget. That is a real loss
     against the intent here and an argument for Pro if socket abuse ever shows
     up in the logs.

     The origin check above is a single header and anyone can send it — this
     repo forges exactly that header itself, deliberately, in
     src/lib/leaderboard.ts. So the origin list is a convention, and the zone
     rate limiting rule is the actual control on a public URL that spends the
     owner's Helius credits.

     Two budgets, because a POST and a socket are not worth the same upstream:
     a POST is billed once, a subscription is billed for as long as it is held.

     THE NUMBERS ARE DELIBERATELY GENEROUS. One pool page fans out into several
     account reads plus a socket, and a household, an office or a campus all
     share one cf-connecting-ip behind NAT. Watch `wrangler tail` for 429s
     before tightening these. Note the binding's counter is approximate and
     per-location rather than global, so treat these as an order of magnitude
     rather than an exact ceiling. */
  "ratelimits": [
    {
      "name": "RPC_LIMIT",
      "namespace_id": "1001",
      "simple": { "limit": 300, "period": 60 }
    },
    {
      "name": "WS_LIMIT",
      "namespace_id": "1002",
      "simple": { "limit": 30, "period": 60 }
    }
  ]
}


